\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Overview of the Simulink model \texttt  {SpaceRobot.slx}   This figure shows the Simulink-based closed-loop simulation framework for the free-floating space robot. The model integrates the robot dynamics, reference generation, observation assembly, reward computation, and a PPO-based reinforcement learning agent that outputs joint torques. The reward and termination logic evaluate end-effector tracking performance, base motion disturbance, and control effort to guide policy learning.}}{6}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spacerobot_slx}{{2}{6}{Overview of the Simulink model \texttt {SpaceRobot.slx} \\ This figure shows the Simulink-based closed-loop simulation framework for the free-floating space robot. The model integrates the robot dynamics, reference generation, observation assembly, reward computation, and a PPO-based reinforcement learning agent that outputs joint torques. The reward and termination logic evaluate end-effector tracking performance, base motion disturbance, and control effort to guide policy learning}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:spacerobot_slx@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][4][]6}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Overview of the Robot Subsystem in the Simulink Model \texttt  {SpaceRobot.slx}   This figure depicts the Simulink/Simscape Multibody model of the free-floating space robot, consisting of a floating base and a serial 4-DOF manipulator. Joint torque commands are applied to the revolute joints, while joint positions and velocities are measured and fed back as state variables. The base orientation is represented as a normalized quaternion and compared to a reference to compute the base attitude error.}}{6}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spacerobot_slx_robot}{{3}{6}{Overview of the Robot Subsystem in the Simulink Model \texttt {SpaceRobot.slx} \\ This figure depicts the Simulink/Simscape Multibody model of the free-floating space robot, consisting of a floating base and a serial 4-DOF manipulator. Joint torque commands are applied to the revolute joints, while joint positions and velocities are measured and fed back as state variables. The base orientation is represented as a normalized quaternion and compared to a reference to compute the base attitude error}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:spacerobot_slx_robot@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][4][]6}{}{}{}}
\citation{b2,b7}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Overview of the Reference Subsystem in the Simulink Model \texttt  {SpaceRobot.slx}  This figure shows the computation of end-effector position and velocity tracking errors with respect to the reference trajectory. The measured end-effector states are compared to the reference signals to generate position and velocity error vectors used for observation and reward calculation. In addition, the base linear and angular velocities are extracted to capture base motion induced by manipulator actuation.}}{7}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spacerobot_slx_reference}{{4}{7}{Overview of the Reference Subsystem in the Simulink Model \texttt {SpaceRobot.slx}\\ This figure shows the computation of end-effector position and velocity tracking errors with respect to the reference trajectory. The measured end-effector states are compared to the reference signals to generate position and velocity error vectors used for observation and reward calculation. In addition, the base linear and angular velocities are extracted to capture base motion induced by manipulator actuation}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:spacerobot_slx_reference@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][4][]7}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{eq:reward}{{1}{7}{Reinforcement Learning Framework}{equation.1}{}}
\newlabel{eq:reward@cref}{{[equation][1][]1}{[1][7][]7}{}{}{}}
